WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:05.060
Tom Holland didn't hit the Hollywood scene
so much as he smashed into it at speed — kind

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:06.600
of like a meteor with charisma.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:10.919
But for all the sparkle of his ride to the
top, there's still a lot we don't know about

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:11.480
the actor.

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:14.780
Here’s the real story behind Marvel’s
youngest leading light.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.860
Apparently, Tom Holland has been dancing since
he was in diapers.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:22.660
In an interview with The Hollywood Reporter,
he recalled that he used to dance to a Janet

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:25.280
Jackson song that his mother would play when
he was a baby.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:29.460
This ended up prompting her to enroll him
in a weekly dance class for children, which

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:33.480
Holland took to immediately — throwing himself
into everything from ballet to hip-hop with

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:34.540
feverish intensity.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.739
And although this was the first step on the
road to becoming one of the world’s most

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:42.240
famous actors, it was also something schoolyard
bullies were quick to jump on at the time.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:44.280
Luckily, as he told People Magazine:

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.140
“You couldn't hit me hard enough to stop
me from doing it."

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:51.620
It was this relentless pursuit of his passion
that pushed him to audition for the titular

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:55.839
role in the West End production of Billy Elliot,
got him doing backflips in his audition tapes

00:00:55.839 --> 00:01:00.190
for Marvel, and ultimately gave him real life
experience as an underdog — something he

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:03.620
now credits as a key inspiration in his performance
as Peter Parker.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.200
Holland told People Magazine:

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:10.000
"I, like Peter, accepted I wasn't the cool
kid at school, and just found my group of

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:11.280
friends and got on with it."

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:15.960
You wouldn't guess it from the way he seems
to revere every new experience fame brings

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:19.960
him, but Tom isn’t the first famous Holland—
nor is he the only one who's worked in the

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:21.080
creative arts.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:26.200
His father Dominic is a comedian, broadcaster,
and author, while his mother Nicola Elizabeth

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:27.060
is a photographer.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:31.280
Despite his early exposure to the world of
performance and his enrollment in that weekly

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:35.060
dance class, Tom's ascent has been described
by his father as a fluke.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.500
“It’s a total fluke what’s happened
to my son Tom.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:39.180
a real fluke.”

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:40.100
“Is it a fluke though?”

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:44.350
Yeah, it has been a fluke because it’s been
no stage school no drama lessons.”

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:48.180
When Tom's agent proposed putting together
an audition tape for Marvel, the family was

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:53.100
roundly skeptical but now that the impossible
has happened, the Hollands credit this unlikely

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:56.160
trajectory as the source of Tom's down-to-earth
nature.

00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:00.380
There are few superhero costumes quite as
iconic as Spider-Man’s.

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:05.060
That's one of the reasons it’s barely changed
since its introduction in 1962 — from the

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:09.690
bold color scheme to the dramatically flared
eyes, it was an instant classic from the moment

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:10.420
it debuted.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:14.420
Putting on that suit has got to be pretty
daunting for any newcomer to the role.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:21.720
“What the hell was that what just happened?

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:23.700
“...You jumped off a sign and fell on your
face.”

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:27.680
But it's not just a figurative burden it's
an entirely literal one, too.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:32.260
In an interview with ShortList, Holland revealed
that it never occured to the crew that he'd

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:37.270
need a way to drink in the suit, a problem
which demanded a complex solution: a hole

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:41.410
is drilled in the eye of the mask, a tube
is fed through, and an assistant squeezes

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:43.000
water into Holland's mouth.

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:46.800
But the consequences of all that guzzling
are just as problematic.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:51.000
To answer nature's call, Holland must strip
down to his regulation underwear — which

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:55.209
is nothing more than a thong — slap on a
bathrobe, and book it to the nearest bathroom

00:02:55.209 --> 00:02:56.840
and back as fast as he can.

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:01.000
However, he’s quick to point out that this
hardship is outweighed by the privilege of

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:03.240
getting to bring the iconic web-slinger to
life.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:08.060
But when you trot off to see him do his thing
in Avengers: Endgame, just remember: the Spider-Man

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:11.940
you’re watching probably just took a sip
through the world's most professional Crazy

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:12.440
Straw.

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:15.480
Everyone knows that Holland is a pretty goofy
guy.

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:19.860
He’s renowned for his playfulness with Marvel
movie spoilers, he's always ready to slap

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:23.599
on some vinyl shorts and perform Rihanna's
"Umbrella" with the best of them, and he can

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:27.440
even find the humor in having to drink through
a straw that someone else operates for him.

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:31.540
But it's not all fun and games — Holland
also has some serious ambitions too.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:32.300
“Alright, kid.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:35.540
You’re an Avenger now.”

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:41.920
He told The Hollywood Reporter:

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:44.300
"The 20-year goal is to be a film director.

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:46.349
The 15-year goal is to win an Oscar.

00:03:46.349 --> 00:03:48.780
The five-year goal is to just keep enjoying
myself."

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:54.400
He also went on to detail his acting role
models, such as Leonardo di Caprio, Jake Gyllenhaal

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:55.140
and Meryl Streep.

00:03:55.849 --> 00:04:00.380
One thing is clear above all else: Holland
wants to be big, he's ready to work for it,

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:03.540
and he has a plan as to just how he's going
to accomplish it.

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:07.819
Holland didn’t expect to ever reach the
peaks of fame he's already conquered, let

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:09.540
alone hit them in his early twenties.

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:13.980
But it really can't be emphasized enough that
up until Captain America: Civil War, in which

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:18.280
Holland made his debut as Spider-Man, he really
thought he'd go on living the life he'd always

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:18.780
led.

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:23.360
Interviews done to promote The Impossible
reveal a 16-year-old Holland still certain

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:27.100
he's going to go back home to Kingston and
finish up high school — while continuing

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:28.760
to pursue his dreams on the side.

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:33.560
Looking back, it’s clear that since Billy
Elliot, Holland has been on a speeding train

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:34.940
to blockbuster success.

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:39.340
After every new production and role, he appears
to have thought that things were going to

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:42.120
go back to normal… except they never really
did.

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:46.580
It's only in recent interviews that Holland
seems to have become someone who truly understands

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:50.160
that his life as he knew it is gone and that
fame is his new normal.

